abernes Selskab. ISBN 978-87-7876-177-4.\n  2. ^ Lange, Dierk (1993). \"Die Hausa-Traditionen in ihrer Abhängigkeit von Kanem-Borno und Nubien\". Anthropos. 88 (1/3): 47–76. ISSN 0257-9774.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKebbi: history, territory and rulers · HistoNaija (https://histonaija.logfolio.pro/kingdoms/kebbi)\nciteturn297search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; \n\nKingdom · 1516–1902\n\n# Kebbi\n\nThe last of the Hausa states to emerge, Kebbi was founded by Muhammadu Kanta, a Katsina-born champion wrestler who became a captain in Songhai's army and rebelled in 1516 over his share of the spoils from Aïr.\n\n## At a glance\n\nWhen did the Kebbi exist?\n    From 1516 to 1902. It was at its height around 1530.\n\nWhat was the capital of the Kebbi?\n    Surame.\n\nWhat was the ruler of the Kebbi called?\n    Kanta, later Sarkin Kebbi.\n\nWhat happened to the Kebbi?\n    The Emir of Argungu, heir of Kanta's line, reigns in Kebbi State; the Argungu fishing festival is held on the river every year.\n\nWho lives in the lands of the Kebbi today?\n    Hausa\n\n## Overview\n\nThe last of the Hausa states to emerge, Kebbi was founded by Muhammadu Kanta, a Katsina-born champion wrestler who became a captain in Songhai's army and rebelled in 1516 over his share of the spoils from Aïr. He beat every Songhai army sent against him, and from his walled cities of Leka, Gungu, Silame and above all Surame he took tribute from much of Hausaland, Aïr, Nupe and Borgu, and held off Bornu. "
